Z||000000||FIEROCIOUS||02-08-2000||01:11 AM||||Why if the clutch system sucks soooooo bad on the fiero has anyone not been able to come up with a new system?<BR>I mean realy no other car has so many differant mods to change say brakes or bodys or interiors even engines! Why "O" why hasn't anyone come up with something better?<BR>I Know us Fiero types can come up with something better!<BR>Has any one thought of a Mcloyd hydralic throwout bearing?  ||||reg||4
Z||000001||william_thorniii||02-08-2000||07:36 PM||||You have got me there... What is a McLeoyd t/o bearing?  Although this could be a neat piece I don't think that it will solve most problems with the clutch system since they are hydraulic.<P>William T. Thorn, III||||reg||5
Z||000002||fiero56||02-08-2000||07:45 PM||||This is just a guess, as I've never heard of them, but I imagine the t/o bearing has the hydraulic line plugged directly into it. Then somehow the bearing expands, pushing the fingers on the pressure plate. Just a guess.||||reg||1
Z||000003||theogre||02-08-2000||08:08 PM||||the integated bearing/piston is about the only thing you could do to it. That is if there is room for it.((We know now not to put petrolium product in it  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> )) ||||reg||1
Z||000004||WKDFIRO||02-08-2000||09:53 PM||||When Fiero Motorsports put a 91 Corvette engine into an 88 GT using Zumalts kit upgrading the clutch was pondered seriously since we were using the Getrag/Muncie 5 speed. We went with a slightly bigger diameter clutch but the V8 ate it in 10K miles. Before we were using the clutch as the weak link because we were afraid of grenading the tranny. The owner didn't care about replacing a tranny and told us to upgrade the clutch. We went with a multidisk clutch that is used in Busch Racing. We tried to break it in gently but found it to be a waste of time. Oddly enough the clutch came to life after we broke it in by beating the hell out of it several times. This was at the owner's suggestion since he used to race Porsche and Ferraris professionally years ago. This made the owner delirious, and the cost of replacing the rear tires (They were turned into rubber suit from the burn outs we were doing...). That Fiero was still able to be docile enough for his wife to drive with out fear. We have no idea why though. We were certain it would be turned into an all or nothing car after that. We were so wrong.<P>Thats our story about upgrading clutches.<p>[This message has been edited by WKDFIRO (edited 02-08-2000).]||||reg||3
Z||000005||FIEROCIOUS||02-09-2000||01:14 AM||||Fiero56 got it! It is a throw out bearing that is hydraulic. Now as I figure the biggest problem is a lack of adjustability right? Well the hydraulic throw bearing is adjustable. The units I've seen are for popular trannies such as the T10 and T5s.<BR>Does anyone have a PAW (performance automotive warehouse) book? I've seen them in the clutch section of the book. I think summit may carry them to! They price around 100.00 but, imagine if you were the first to make a kit! HOTCAKES! The only other problem is they have two lines going to it one braided and one solid (in out?) don't know if it has an integral releif valve?||||reg||10
Z||000006||2birds||02-10-2000||09:33 PM||||Summit sells them for circle track cars, and I know of two vehicles that use this type of throwout bearing from the factory; Ford F-series and T-bird Super Coupes.   It bolts to the transmission and pushes against the diaphragm.  The problem I see is bleeding the system in a transaxle bellhousing without access holes.  What do you gain, except maybe room for exhaust/turbo mods?<p>[This message has been edited by 2birds (edited 02-10-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000007||pherder||02-10-2000||09:53 PM||||Well, I can name one other car that uses a hydraulic throwout bearing and that is the later model Quad 4s with a GETRAG transaxle!!!<P>George Ryan is using one in he 84 Fiero race car w/Q4.  He had to go to a larger clutch master cylinder to push enough fluid to disenage the clutch but it is running great now!  <P>The two lines are pressure in and bleed (I think ...  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>Paul||||reg||4
